<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicene Christianity</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicene <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> is the Christian doctrine defined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a> in 325 and the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 381, which holds that the Son is of the same substance as the Father. Made the official faith of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> in 380, it was opposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>, which many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, had adopted. Conflict between the two shaped the kingdoms that succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity.jpg" alt="Nicene Christianity" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicaea_icon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
